---
/**
 * The page's floor, and nothing more than that. Three things: the
 * lockup, the social icons, the copyright line.
 *
 * WHY SO LITTLE. Its one real job is to END THE PAGE. Everything above
 * sits on PageBackdrop's drifting ground, which has no bottom edge of
 * its own — scroll to the end of the home page without this and the last
 * panel simply stops. A solid band is what says the document is over,
 * and a band can do that while carrying almost nothing.
 *
 * So the things a footer usually accumulates are all gone:
 *
 * - NO SITE MAP. The original reason for this is gone — the nav entries
 *   are six real pages now, not same-page anchors, so a footer nav
 *   would take you somewhere rather than back up the page you just
 *   read. The decision stands on a different footing: the header is
 *   STICKY, so the identical six links are on screen at the moment you
 *   reach the bottom. A footer sitemap here is a second copy of a menu
 *   that never left. Revisit it if the header ever stops sticking, or
 *   if the site grows a tier of pages the nav doesn't carry.
 * - NO CONTACT COLUMN. Phone and email belong to the contact section,
 *   which is a destination with room to say more than a list of facts.
 *   The whatsapp icon is the one shortcut worth keeping down here.
 * - NO BLURB. The page above just spent four paragraphs saying it.
 *
 * LAYOUT: three grid columns from `md`, with the third left empty on
 * purpose. That's what puts the icons at the true centre of the band
 * rather than at the centre of the space the lockup leaves over — the
 * arrangement the reference footer uses, and it only holds if the row
 * is a fixed three-column track. Under `md` the same two children stay
 * on ONE line, pushed to the two edges — the header's arrangement, and
 * the reason the lockup sheds its wordmark down there (below). Stacking
 * them would spend two rows of a narrow screen on a mark and two icons.
 * The copyright sits below the rule, centred at every width.
 *
 * SURFACE: WHITE, LIKE THE HEADER, and for the same reason Header
 * gives: white is the surface every card on the page already uses, so
 * the two bars read as the topmost and bottommost of them, and the
 * shadow is what says they float over the ground rather than being
 * painted onto it. The header's own shadow falls downward onto the page;
 * this one is `--shadow-card-up`, the same token mirrored, so it falls
 * upward onto the page from below. The pair is what brackets the
 * document.
 *
 * (It was `paper-100` first — the drawer's tone, on the argument that a
 * footer is chrome. But paper-100 is the tone of chrome that OPENS OVER
 * the page; a tinted band at the bottom of a page whose ground is
 * already a colour wash just reads as one more zone of it.)
 *
 * No top margin: the section above ends on its own `py-section`, and a
 * second gap on top of that leaves the footer floating rather than
 * closing the page.
 *
 * `layout/` is where this belongs once the proposed direction ships
 * (structural chrome shared across pages, next to Header and SideNav) —
 * it's here for now because everything it's built from is.
 */
import Logo from './Logo.astro';
import FacebookIcon from '../../assets/icons/facebook.svg';
import WhatsappIcon from '../../assets/icons/whatsapp.svg';
import { currentLocale, ui } from '../../data/i18n';
import { hasWhatsapp, homeHref, siteConfig, whatsappHref } from '../../data/site';

const locale = currentLocale(Astro);
const t = ui(locale);

/*
 * The icon row. Both icons always render; each one's URL degrades to the
 * on-page contact section while the field behind it is a TODO, which is
 * the fallback `whatsappHref()` already takes for the header CTA. So an
 * icon is never a dead link, and never silently missing either — the
 * footer looks the same before and after site.ts gets the real URLs.
 */
const facebook = siteConfig.social.facebook;

const socials = [
  {
    label: hasWhatsapp ? t.whatsapp : t.contactNoun,
    href: whatsappHref(),
    Icon: WhatsappIcon,
    /* Only a real off-site URL opens a new tab. */
    external: hasWhatsapp,
  },
  {
    label: facebook ? t.facebook : t.contactNoun,
    href: facebook || '#contact',
    Icon: FacebookIcon,
    external: Boolean(facebook),
  },
];

/* Built at build time, so it's the year the site was last deployed.
   Good enough for a copyright line and cheaper than shipping a script. */
const year = new Date().getFullYear();
---

<footer class="bg-white shadow-card-up">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-[var(--width-content)] px-gutter py-10 lg:px-gutter-lg lg:py-12">
    <div
      class="flex items-center justify-between gap-6 md:grid md:grid-cols-3 md:items-center"
    >
      <!-- `variant="responsive"`, the same as the header: below `sm` the
           wordmark drops and only the petals are left. That's what keeps
           the lockup and the icons on one line on a phone instead of the
           wordmark eating the width and forcing them onto two — and it
           means the top and the bottom of the page shed their labels at
           the same breakpoint rather than at two different ones. The
           mark isn't orphaned down here the way it would be alone: the
           copyright line below it spells the name out in full.

           `tone="mono"` puts the whole lockup — petals included — in one
           flat ink instead of the four brand colours. The page above is
           already carrying the palette; a full-colour mark down here is
           a fifth bright thing competing with it, when the footer's job
           is to close the page quietly. The ink is `text-ink-900`, which
           tokens.css defines as `#243C5A` — the wordmark's own colour,
           so the mono lockup is the artwork's navy applied to all of it
           rather than a new colour. -->
      <Logo class="h-14 text-ink-900 lg:h-16" href={homeHref(locale)} variant="responsive" tone="mono" />

      <!-- `justify-self-center` centres the list inside the middle
           track; the track itself is what centres it on the band. -->
      <ul class="flex items-center gap-4 md:justify-self-center">
        {
          socials.map(({ label, href, Icon, external }) => (
            <li>
              <a
                href={href}
                class="inline-flex text-ink-900 transition-colors duration-[var(--duration-fast)] hover:text-teal-800"
                {...(external ? { target: '_blank', rel: 'noopener noreferrer' } : {})}
              >
                {/* The glyphs are decorative; the link's name is the
                    text beside them, which is why they're the ones
                    carrying aria-hidden and this span is sr-only. */}
                <span class="sr-only">{label}</span>
                <Icon class="h-7 w-7" aria-hidden="true" />
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <!-- Third column left empty on purpose — see the header comment.
           It's what makes the middle track the centre of the band. -->
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 border-t border-ink-900/10 pt-6 text-center text-sm text-ink-600">
      © {year} {siteConfig.name}. {t.rightsReserved}
    </p>
  </div>
</footer>
